1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:08,618
Embedding capacity is scaling alongside Transformer computation, making high compression rates increasingly useful.

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:22,214
E2B and E4B are two models in the Gemma 4 family. PLE accounts for 45.8% and 35.2% of their parameters.

3
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:28,217
PLE stores a d-dimensional vector for each token at each decoder layer.

4
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:31,028
A token's vectors can differ across layers.

5
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:37,786
OPTIC compresses this V by L by d tensor, keeping the backbone fixed.

6
00:00:37,899 --> 00:00:42,675
A fixed index budget per token is split across L independent layer codes.

7
00:00:42,799 --> 00:00:47,895
OPTIC pools that budget into one larger code tuple, shared across layers.

8
00:00:47,999 --> 00:00:57,416
Layer-specific decoders preserve layer differences. All V tokens share each decoder, so its storage is independent of V.

9
00:00:57,532 --> 00:01:00,864
Each index selects a codeword from its group's codebook.

10
00:01:00,965 --> 00:01:04,145
The selected codewords are reused at every layer.

11
00:01:04,265 --> 00:01:13,122
Concatenating M codewords of width d_c gives a latent of width Z = M d_c.

12
00:01:13,232 --> 00:01:19,846
Each layer reconstructs its PLE vector with an affine decoder: A times z, plus b.

13
00:01:19,965 --> 00:01:28,022
The latent width Z can exceed d; each layer can still use a full-rank decoder.

14
00:01:28,132 --> 00:01:32,592
Every layer uses the same latent with its own decoder weights and bias.

15
00:01:32,798 --> 00:01:39,254
The model uses a PLE vector through a context-dependent gate and a layer-specific output projection.

16
00:01:39,365 --> 00:01:45,338
OPTIC fits the projected contribution, before normalization and residual addition.

17
00:01:45,465 --> 00:01:52,640
Compare the original and reconstructed contributions under the same cached gates and frozen projections.

18
00:01:52,765 --> 00:01:58,424
Fit the shared codebooks and layer decoders to minimize their mean squared difference.

19
00:01:58,565 --> 00:02:05,023
With indices fixed, optimize codebooks, decoder weights and biases under the output-path loss.

20
00:02:05,131 --> 00:02:11,670
Then fix those parameters and reassign indices group by group, using a path-derived average metric.

21
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:23,932
E4B uses 161 eight-bit indices per token, forming a 644-dimensional latent for 42 layers, each producing 256 coordinates.

22
00:02:24,048 --> 00:02:38,745
Including codes, codebooks and decoders, resident PLE storage falls from 5.64 GB to 56.4 MB: about 100 times smaller.

23
00:02:38,865 --> 00:02:47,884
Performance remains close to the original: 97.8% aggregate retention across seven benchmarks on BF16 E4B.

24
00:02:48,015 --> 00:02:55,672
Inference decodes only the current tokens. At the same cache size, inference time is essentially unchanged.
